# 12_LIBRARY_EXPERIENCE

Version: 1.0
Status: Draft
Project: BinderHaven
Company: Poole Labs

---

# Purpose

The Library is the heart of BinderHaven.

It is more than a list of collections.

It is the collector's personal gallery.

Every visit to BinderHaven begins here.

The Library should immediately communicate ownership, progress, and pride while encouraging collectors to continue building their collections.

Collectors should enjoy spending time in the Library even when they are not actively managing cards.

---

# Experience Philosophy

The Library should never feel like inventory software.

It should never resemble a spreadsheet.

It should never overwhelm the collector with statistics.

Instead, it should recreate the feeling of standing in front of a personal collection.

Every binder represents an achievement.

Every collection tells a story.

The Library exists to celebrate those stories.

---

# Emotional Objectives

When a collector opens BinderHaven they should immediately think:

• This feels different.

• This feels premium.

• This doesn't look like every other collection app.

• I want to open one of these binders.

The Library should create curiosity before interaction.

---

# Layout Philosophy

The Library is intentionally simple.

Collectors browse binders—not lists.

The default layout consists of:

• Two binders per row

• Vertical scrolling

• Generous spacing

• Large binder covers

The binders are always the primary visual element.

Nothing should compete for attention.

---

# Wallpaper Philosophy

The wallpaper establishes the atmosphere of the Library.

It creates mood without distracting from the binders.

The wallpaper is not decoration.

It is environmental design.

---

# Default Wallpapers

BinderHaven includes a curated collection of professionally designed wallpapers.

Examples include:

• Minimal gradients

• Nature landscapes

• Dark premium textures

• Collector-inspired environments

• Seasonal collections

Every wallpaper should complement binder covers rather than compete with them.

---

# Personal Wallpapers

Collectors may choose their own wallpapers.

Examples include:

• Personal photography

• Family photos

• Landscapes

• Abstract artwork

• Licensed artwork they own the rights to use

To maintain readability, BinderHaven may automatically apply subtle visual treatments such as:

• Blur

• Dark overlays

• Brightness adjustment

• Reduced saturation

The objective is to preserve binder visibility regardless of wallpaper selection.

---

# Wallpaper Principle

The wallpaper sets the mood.

The binders own the attention.

---

# Binder Presentation

Binders are displayed as premium physical objects.

Each binder should communicate:

• Collection name

• Completion percentage

• Cards owned

• Cards remaining

• Current Set Value

The binder itself remains the hero.

Information should support the object rather than dominate it.

---

# Information Hierarchy

Information should always appear in the following priority:

1. Collection Name

2. Completion Percentage

3. Cards Owned

4. Cards Remaining

5. Current Set Value

Example:

Pokémon Base Set

82% Complete

643 / 780 Cards

137 Remaining

Current Set Value

$2,438.17

Progress should always receive greater visual emphasis than monetary value.

BinderHaven celebrates collecting—not investing.

---

# Header

The Library intentionally avoids personal greetings.

Examples such as:

"Good Morning"

"Welcome Back"

"Happy Collecting"

should not appear.

Instead, the experience begins with a simple title.

Library

The focus immediately shifts to the collector's binders.

---

# Navigation Philosophy

Navigation should feel integrated into the BinderHaven identity.

Rather than relying exclusively on traditional bottom navigation, BinderHaven explores a persistent navigation rail positioned along the left edge of the interface.

This navigation becomes a recognizable element of the product and subtly reinforces the physical inspiration of a binder spine.

The navigation should remain minimal, elegant, and unobtrusive.

It should support the experience without competing for attention.

---

# Spacing Philosophy

Whitespace should be intentional.

The Library should breathe.

Spacing should:

• Improve readability

• Increase perceived quality

• Draw attention toward binders

Whitespace exists to elevate the collecting experience—not simply to create empty space.

---

# Visual Hierarchy

Collectors should notice elements in this order:

Wallpaper

↓

Binder Covers

↓

Collection Names

↓

Progress

↓

Supporting Statistics

↓

Navigation

Every design decision should reinforce this hierarchy.

---

# Motion Philosophy

Scrolling should feel smooth and calm.

Binders should appear stable and substantial.

Interactions should communicate weight and physical presence.

Animations should never distract from collecting.

The Library should feel confident rather than energetic.

---

# Recognition

The Library should become instantly recognizable.

If someone sees a screenshot online, they should recognize BinderHaven before reading the title.

Recognition should come from:

• Premium binder presentation

• Calm visual hierarchy

• Wallpaper treatment

• Navigation

• Overall composition

Not logos.

Not branding.

---

# Accessibility

The Library should remain readable across all supported wallpapers.

Text should always maintain sufficient contrast.

Interactive elements should remain comfortably reachable.

Large binder covers should remain visually distinguishable for all users.

Customization should never reduce usability.

---

# Success Criteria

A successful Library experience should:

• Feel unlike traditional collection apps

• Encourage collectors to spend time browsing

• Celebrate collection progress

• Make every binder feel valuable

• Support personalization without sacrificing clarity

• Scale naturally as collections grow

• Become one of the defining experiences of BinderHaven

If collectors enjoy opening BinderHaven simply to admire their Library, the design has succeeded.